### 🔍 Why Timsort Wins on This Exact Profile
1. **Run Detection Exploits Partial Sorting**: Timsort scans for ascending/descending runs in O(n) time. Your data's "partially sorted with few inversion clusters" means it will identify long runs and merge them with minimal comparisons/swaps. Effective complexity approaches **O(n)**, not O(n log n).
2. **Dual-Pivot QS Suffers on Nearly-Sorted Data**: Even with dual pivots, partitioning nearly-sorted data creates highly unbalanced subarrays unless pivots are median-of-k or introsort-fallback is triggered. You pay O(n log n) comparisons + cache thrashing from recursive partitioning, plus extra swaps for the inversion clusters.
3. **Branch Prediction & Cache Behavior**: Timsort's merge step is sequential and prediction-friendly. QS's recursive partitioning scatters memory accesses, causing more L1 misses and branch mispredictions on this data shape.
4. **Predictability**: Timsort's worst-case is guaranteed O(n log n) with low constant factors. Dual-pivot QS can spike to O(n²) on pathological nearly-sorted inputs unless hardened with introsort/randomization, which adds overhead you don't need here.

